Not exactly.

Let me try an analogy.

In the widespread competitive card game competition known as
"duplicate contract bridge", where each team plays the same
deal of cards sa all other pairs in their "direction" against
some other team at least once, there is a strategy of
desperation called "tops and bottoms".

It is resorted to when the play of a partnership partway
through a tournament looks to be far below a winning level.

"Okay, we aren't winning on merit. Can we win on luck?"

And so the partnership makes risky bids that other pairs
still firmly in the competition would avoid. They make bids
that will either get them a "top" score if the card deal has
some unexpected peculiarities, or a "bottom" score if the
deal is a more normally distributed one.

The way Duplicate Bridge is scored, even a couple more
good results than bad results from this high risk strategy
can cause a team to win the tournament.  The more stable
parnerships _never_ use such a strategy, and roundly despise
those who do and sometimes deprive the more pedestrian players
of a deserved tournament win by luck rather than skill.

Drinking from fountains, quaffing unIDed potions, reading
unIDed scrolls, zapping unIDed wands at monsters, sitting on
thrones, all "to see what happens" in the hope it is one of
the favorable outcomes, is playing NetHack "tops and bottoms".

You might win once in a while, but over time and over the long
length of a game of NetHack, you'll lose on average by choosing
high risk strategies in situations where you have any other
choice. Playing with skill and knowledge to minimize risk versus
gain is a superior long run choice to playing by gambling.

It doesn't matter in any of these cases even that there is a
balance of outcomes that are favorable, it matters that there
are some which are plainly disasterous, and if you take such
risks often enough, you'll "roll snakeyes" sooner or later in
a long enough series.

